## Why This Is Asked
Dynamic connectivity with insertions and deletions is a classic hard problem. This offline approach tests understanding of edge lifetimes and versioning, and how to combine segment trees with DSU rollback for log-scale updates.

## Key Concepts
- DSU with rollback (no path compression) to enable undo.
- Segment tree over time to map edge active intervals to time ranges.
- Offline processing: traverse tree, apply unions, answer leaf queries, then rollback.

## Code Example
```javascript
class RollbackDSU {
  constructor(n){
    this.parent = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i);
    this.sz = Array(n).fill(1);
    this.ch = [];
  }
  find(x){ while (this.parent[x]!==x) x = this.parent[x]; return x; }
  union(a,b){
    a = this.find(a); b = this.find(b);
    if (a===b){ this.ch.push(null); return; }
    if (this.sz[a] < this.sz[b]) [a,b] = [b,a];
    this.ch.push([b, this.parent[b], a, this.sz[a]]);
    this.parent[b] = a; this.sz[a] += this.sz[b];
  }
  snapshot(){ return this.ch.length; }
  rollback(snap){
    while (this.ch.length > snap){
      const ch = this.ch.pop();
      if (!ch) continue;
      const [b, oldP, a, oldS] = ch;
      this.parent[b] = oldP; this.sz[a] = oldS;
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests augmented BST design for duplicates and range queries on a dynamic multiset, focusing on balance, memory, and correctness.

## Key Concepts
- Treap with duplicates via per-node cnt
- Subtree size and sum augmentation
- kth smallest by counts; range queries via splits or traversal

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node{constructor(key,prio){this.key=key;this.prio=prio;this.cnt=1;this.l=null;this.r=null;this.sz=1;this.sum=key}};
function upd(n){if(!n)return; n.sz=n.cnt+(n.l?.sz||0)+(n.r?.sz||0); n.sum=n.key*n.cnt+(n.l?.sum||0)+(n.r?.sum||0)}
function rotateRight(y){const x=y.l; y.l=x.r; x.r=y; upd(y); upd(x); return x}
function rotateLeft(x){const y=x.r; x.r=y.l; y.l=x; upd(x); upd(y); return y}
function insert(n, key){if(!n)return new Node(key,Math.random()); if(key===n.key){n.cnt++; upd(n);return n}; if(key<n.key){n.l=insert(n.l,key); if(n.l.prio>n.prio) n=rotateRight(n);} else {n.r=insert(n.r,key); if(n.r.prio>n.prio) n=rotateLeft(n);} upd(n); return n}
function kth(n,k){if(!n) return null; const leftSize=n.l?.sz||0; if(k<=leftSize) return kth(n.l,k); if(k>leftSize+n.cnt) return kth(n.r,k-leftSize-n.cnt); return n.key;}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Dynamic string editing with fast substring queries is critical in modern editors and versioned docs. A rope with an implicit treap provides randomized balance and localized updates, enabling logarithmic edits while maintaining per-substring hashes for quick comparisons.

## Key Concepts

- Implicit treap (rope) structure for sequence storage
- Subtree size and rolling hash maintenance
- Polynomial rolling hash with two moduli to reduce collisions
- Split and merge operations for positional edits
- Substring hash isolation without copying strings

## Code Example

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(ch) {
    this.c = ch;
    this.pr = Math.random();
    this.left = null; this.right = null;
    this.sz = 1;
    this.h1 = ch.charCodeAt(0);
    this.h2 = ch.charCodeAt(0);
  }
}
// Update size and hashes from children
function upd(n){ if(!n) return; n.sz = 1; n.h1 = n.c.charCodeAt(0); n.h2 = n.c.charCodeAt(0);
  if(n.left){ n.sz += n.left.sz; n.h1 = n.left.h1 + somePow1; n.h2 = n.left.h2 + somePow2; }
  if(n.right){ n.sz += n.right.sz; n.h1 = n.h1 + rightHash1; n.h2 = n.h2 + rightHash2; }
}
function split(t, k){ // split first k chars to a
  if(!t) return [null, null];
  if(size(t.left) >= k){ const [l, r] = split(t.left, k); t.left = r; upd(t); return [l, t]; }
  const [l, r] = split(t.right, k - size(t.left) - 1); t.right = l; upd(t); return [t, r];
}
function merge(a, b){ if(!a||!b) return a||b; if(a.pr < b.pr){ a.right = merge(a.right, b); upd(a); return a; } else { b.left = merge(a, b.left); upd(b); return b; } }
```

